   119  /**
   120   * Server Network Layer
   121   * --------------------
   122   * The server exposes two functions to interact with remote clients:
   123   * `processBinaryMessage` and `removeConnection`. These functions are called by
   124   * the server network layer.
   125   *
   126   * It is the job of the server network layer to listen on a TCP socket, to
   127   * accept new connections, to call the server with received messages and to
   128   * signal closed connections to the server.
   129   *
   130   * The network layer is part of the server config. So users can provide a custom
   131   * implementation if the provided example does not fit their architecture. The
   132   * network layer is invoked only from the server's main loop. So the network
   133   * layer does not need to be thread-safe. If the networklayer receives a
   134   * positive duration for blocking listening, the server's main loop will block
   135   * until a message is received or the duration times out. */
   136
   137  /* Process a binary message (TCP packet). The message can contain partial
   138   * chunks. (TCP is a streaming protocol and packets may be split/merge during
   139   * transport.) After processing, the message is freed with
   140   * connection->releaseRecvBuffer. */
   141  void UA_EXPORT
   142  UA_Server_processBinaryMessage(UA_Server *server, UA_Connection *connection,
   143                                 UA_ByteString *message);
